[HOLE 4] Per-lane RX pad->capture delay cap — STAGED, GUARDED OFF, NOT CLOSED

STATUS: NOT CLOSED. This patch delivers the constraint, the derivation and the
        experiment; it does NOT deliver a landable change for the frozen run.
        The block it adds is guarded OFF by an env var and prints a NOTE saying
        so. Applying this patch changes no timing. Read WHAT IS MISSING below.

WHAT THIS DOES
    Appends a guarded block to tidelink_constraints.sdc.
      guard unset (default)  -> 0 constraints, one NOTE line in the log
      TL_RX_DATAPATH_CAP=1   -> 8 x set_max_delay -ignore_clock_latency,
                                one per lane, 16 endpoints each

WHY IT IS NOT SIMPLY ON — THREE FINDINGS, TWO SETTLED AND ONE NOT

  1. -datapath_only DOES NOT EXIST IN EITHER TOOL HERE.  SETTLED.
     Genus 21.1 SDC Command Reference lists set_max_delay's options as
     [-rise] [-fall] [-ignore_clock_latency] [-exception_name] [-comment]
     [-combinational_from_to] [-reset_path]; "datapath_only" occurs ZERO times
     in the whole document. Innovus 21.11 TCR: `set_max_delay value
     [-combinational_from_to | -ignore_clock_latency]`. The block SDC is right
     that it is PrimeTime-only and right that -ignore_clock_latency is the
     Cadence spelling — but they are not equivalent, which is finding 2.

  2. -ignore_clock_latency EXCLUDES CLOCK LATENCY AND NOTHING ELSE.  SETTLED.
     Two Innovus globals control the rest and BOTH DEFAULT TO false:
       timing_constraint_path_delay_exclude_io_delay_from_ignore_clock_latency
       timing_constraint_path_delay_exclude_check_delay_from_ignore_clock_latency
     So at defaults the set_input_delay -max is charged INSIDE the budget. A
     bare 2.0 ns cap anchored at [get_ports {TL_RX[*]}] alongside a 2.5 ns
     input delay violates by >= 0.5 ns on every lane before any silicon delay —
     an unfixable false violation. The budget in this patch adds it back:
     D2D_RX_DP_MAX = T_UI/5 + D2D_RX_IN_MAX = 2.0 + 2.5 = 4.5 ns.

  3. WHETHER set_max_delay DISPLACES THE CLOCK-DERIVED HOLD CHECK. **NOT
     SETTLED, AND IT IS THE BLOCKER.**
     Innovus's reference says set_max_delay "takes precedence over other path
     exceptions specified for the same path, except for the set_false_path
     command", so a path-delay constraint replaces the clock-derived check on
     TL_RX[*] -> capture — the SAME paths the RX input-delay window (hole1)
     exists to constrain.
       SETUP is safe, arithmetically: the clock-derived check allows
       10.0 - 2.5 - uncertainty - t_setup ~ 7.4 ns of datapath; this bound
       allows 2.0. Strictly tighter, never looser.
       HOLD is not stated in EITHER reference. If the min requirement silently
       defaults to 0, this block DELETES the -min that hole1 adds — the one
       constraint here whose absence is a silicon failure that slowing the link
       down does not recover. A budget assertion that quietly removes a safety
       check is worse than no constraint.
     AND IT CANNOT BE PAPERED OVER. The hold requirement hole1 creates is
     (clock insertion + t_hold + 0.4 ns); clock insertion is not expressible in
     SDC, so there is no companion set_min_delay that reproduces it.

WHAT IS MISSING, PLAINLY
    One measurement, on any build that already carries hole1, before and after
    setting TL_RX_DATAPATH_CAP=1:

        report_timing -hold -from [get_ports {TL_RX[0]}] \
            -to [get_pins <GPIO>/gpiorx_0/link_data_pad_clk_reg[0]/D]

    Hold path still reported, required time still derived from D2D_RX_CLK_0 and
    the -0.4 ns arrival  ->  benign; delete the guard and land it.
    Hold check gone, or required time collapsed to 0  ->  do not use this form.
    The alternative is then to set both globals above to true in the P&R setup
    and re-derive the budget as the bare 2.0 ns. That is a flow-script change
    with effects beyond this constraint and belongs in its own reviewed patch,
    not smuggled into an SDC.

    Note the globals affect ONLY paths carrying -ignore_clock_latency, so they
    would not disturb the C2 block at constraints.sdc:568-577 (whose
    -datapath_only is rejected and whose catch fallback is a PLAIN
    set_max_delay). That is a convenience, not a licence to skip the review.

WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM
    2.0 ns = T_UI/5 at T_UI = 10.0 ns.
    Block SDC:74  RX_DATAPATH_MAX_NS = T_UI_NS/5.
    Block SDC:66-70 gives the ratio's provenance: "the same ratios validated on
    FPGA at T_UI=40 ns (set_max_delay 8 ns ~ T_UI/5 ...) scaled down to ASIC
    T_UI". Chain: FPGA-validated 8 ns at 40 ns UI -> ratio 1/5 -> 2.0 ns here.
    +2.5 ns for the charged input delay, per finding 2. The capture setup arc
    (~0.05-0.10 ns, tcbn65lp DFF) is inside the 4.5 and is documented rather
    than padded out.

    THIS IS A DIFFERENT CONSTRAINT FROM THE ONE AT constraints.sdc:568-570.
    That is C2 Option A's payload bound: different source (a2l_fc_replay FIFO
    and addrsync cells), different target (the TX clock group and $EXTCLK),
    different value ($EXTCLK_PERIOD). Nothing in this patch touches it.

TARGET, MEASURED IN THE SHIPPING NETLIST
    gpiorx_<0..7>/link_data_pad_clk_reg[15:0] — 16 per lane, 128 total. It is
    the per-lane deserialiser: each bit loads io_pad when adj_count == n
    (WavD2DGpioRx_v2.v:402-424), i.e. the true pad capture register. Same
    selector shape the block SDC uses, with a candidate ladder for the pre-map
    spelling and error-on-zero.

APPLIES TO
    ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/tidelink_constraints.sdc, AFTER hole1 and hole2.
    It uses $D2D_T_UI and $D2D_RX_IN_MAX, which hole1 introduces, and appends
    after hole2's block. Apply order: hole1 -> hole2 -> hole4.

APPLY
    git apply ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/patches/hole1-d2d-io-delay-window.patch
    git apply ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/patches/hole2-deskew-fifo-cdc.patch
    git apply ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/patches/hole4-rx-datapath-cap-STAGED.patch

NOT VALIDATED
    No EDA run was made. Findings 1 and 2 are quoted from the installed Genus
    21.1 and Innovus 21.11 references. Finding 3 is absent from both references
    and was NOT resolved by experiment.

--- a/ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/tidelink_constraints.sdc
+++ b/ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/tidelink_constraints.sdc
@@ -1130,3 +1130,133 @@
 # D2D_RX_WORDN_CLK_n -> D2D_RX_WORD_CLK_n half-cycle check inside
 # WavD2DGpioRx (link_data_reg -> the FIFO write port), which is a genuine
 # 80 ns synchronous check and must stay timed.
+
+# ===========================================================================
+# [HOLE 4] PER-LANE RX PAD->CAPTURE DELAY CAP — STAGED, NOT FOR THE FROZEN RUN
+# ===========================================================================
+# ###########################################################################
+# ## DO NOT LAND THIS IN THE SAME RUN AS THE RX INPUT-DELAY WINDOW ABOVE.   ##
+# ## The whole block is guarded OFF by default. Read the collision first.   ##
+# ###########################################################################
+#
+# WHAT IT IS. TideLink's block SDC caps each lane's absolute pad->capture delay
+# at RX_DATAPATH_MAX_NS = T_UI/5 (its lines 74 and 152-165) and the chiplet has
+# no equivalent. At this die's T_UI of 10.0 ns the ratio gives 2.0 ns.
+#
+# WHY IT IS NOT SIMPLY ENABLED. Two tool facts and one unresolved interaction.
+#
+# FACT 1 - -datapath_only DOES NOT EXIST IN EITHER TOOL HERE. Verified against
+# the shipping references. Genus 21.1 SDC Command Reference lists set_max_delay
+# as [-rise] [-fall] [-ignore_clock_latency] [-exception_name] [-comment]
+# [-combinational_from_to] [-reset_path]; the string "datapath_only" occurs ZERO
+# times in that document. Innovus 21.11 TCR gives `set_max_delay value
+# [-combinational_from_to | -ignore_clock_latency]`. The block SDC's own note is
+# right that it is PrimeTime-only, and right that -ignore_clock_latency is the
+# substitute -- but the substitute is NOT equivalent, which is FACT 2.
+#
+# FACT 2 - -ignore_clock_latency EXCLUDES CLOCK LATENCY AND NOTHING ELSE. Two
+# Innovus globals control the rest and BOTH DEFAULT TO false:
+#   timing_constraint_path_delay_exclude_io_delay_from_ignore_clock_latency
+#       "Default: false ... When set to true, the software excludes the
+#        input/output delays from slack computation for paths constrained by
+#        the set_max_delay / set_min_delay -ignore_clock_latency command."
+#   timing_constraint_path_delay_exclude_check_delay_from_ignore_clock_latency
+#       "Default: false ... excludes check arc delays"
+# So at defaults, a bound of 2.0 ns anchored at [get_ports {TL_RX[*]}] is
+# charged the set_input_delay -max FIRST. With the 2.5 ns -max above, every one
+# of the 8 lanes violates by >= 0.5 ns before a single picosecond of silicon
+# delay -- an unfixable false violation on the interface with the known
+# marginal-eye failure mode. The budget below therefore ADDS the input delay
+# back in; see D2D_RX_DP_MAX.
+#
+# THE UNRESOLVED INTERACTION - AND IT IS WHY THIS IS OFF, NOT WHY IT IS SLOW.
+# Innovus's set_max_delay reference: "The set_max_delay command takes precedence
+# over other path exceptions specified for the same path, except for the
+# set_false_path command." A path-delay constraint on TL_RX[*] -> capture
+# REPLACES the clock-derived setup check on exactly the paths the RX input-delay
+# window above exists to constrain.
+#
+#   For SETUP that is SAFE and can be shown arithmetically: the clock-derived
+#   check allows 10.0 - 2.5 - uncertainty - t_setup ~ 7.4 ns of datapath, and
+#   this bound allows 2.0. Strictly tighter, never looser -- the same argument
+#   the C2 block makes for its catch fallback (constraints.sdc:545-548).
+#
+#   For HOLD IT IS NOT SETTLED, AND HOLD IS THE HALF THAT MATTERS. Neither the
+#   Genus nor the Innovus reference states whether a path carrying
+#   set_max_delay still takes the clock-derived hold check, or whether the min
+#   requirement silently defaults to 0. If it defaults to 0, THIS BLOCK DELETES
+#   THE -min FROM THE RX WINDOW ABOVE -- the one constraint in this file whose
+#   absence is a silicon failure that no amount of slowing the link down
+#   recovers. Landing a budget assertion that quietly removes a safety check is
+#   strictly worse than landing nothing.
+#
+#   AND IT CANNOT BE PAPERED OVER WITH A COMPANION set_min_delay. The hold
+#   requirement the window creates is (clock insertion + t_hold + 0.4 ns), and
+#   clock insertion is not expressible in SDC. There is no equivalent min-delay
+#   number to write.
+#
+# THE EXPERIMENT THAT SETTLES IT, AND IT IS CHEAP. On any build that already
+# has the RX window from the block above, before and after flipping the guard:
+#     report_timing -hold -from [get_ports {TL_RX[0]}] \
+#         -to [get_pins <GPIO>/gpiorx_0/link_data_pad_clk_reg[0]/D]
+# If the hold path is still reported with a required time derived from
+# D2D_RX_CLK_0 and the -0.4 ns arrival, the interaction is benign and this
+# block can be turned on permanently. If the hold check disappears or its
+# required time collapses to 0, it must NOT be used in this form -- the
+# alternative is to set the two globals above to true in the P&R setup and
+# re-derive the budget as the bare 2.0 ns, which is a flow-script change and
+# belongs in a separate, reviewed patch.
+#
+# WHY ANCHORING AT THE PAD PIN INSTEAD DOES NOT ESCAPE THIS. Moving -from to
+# uPAD_TL_RX_<n>/C removes the input-delay term, but -from on a non-startpoint
+# pin SEGMENTS the timing path, which removes the port-launched path the RX
+# window is checked on. Same loss, different mechanism.
+
+# GUARD. Off unless the run explicitly asks for it. Deliberately an env var and
+# not a bare `if {0}`: an env var leaves a trace in the run's config capture,
+# so a build made with this on can be told apart from one made without it.
+if {[info exists ::env(TL_RX_DATAPATH_CAP)] && $::env(TL_RX_DATAPATH_CAP) ne "0"} {
+
+  # 2.0 ns of true datapath (T_UI/5, the block SDC's ratio at this die's UI)
+  # PLUS the -max input delay, because the globals above default to charging it.
+  # The capture flop's setup arc is charged too and is inside this number
+  # (~0.05-0.10 ns for a tcbn65lp DFF), so the true datapath headroom is
+  # ~1.9 ns, not 2.0. Documented rather than padded: padding it would make the
+  # constraint agree with itself instead of with the silicon.
+  set D2D_RX_DP_MAX [expr {$D2D_T_UI / 5.0 + $D2D_RX_IN_MAX}]
+
+  set _dp_n 0
+  foreach n {0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7} {
+      set _dp_c {}
+      foreach _c [list "$GPIO/gpiorx_$n/link_data_pad_clk_reg*" \
+                       "$GPIO/gpiorx_$n/link_data_pad_clk*"] {
+          set _dp_c [get_cells -quiet $_c]
+          if {[sizeof_collection $_dp_c] > 0} { break }
+      }
+      if {[sizeof_collection $_dp_c] == 0} {
+          error "tidelink_constraints.sdc: RX capture flop selector for lane $n\
+ matched NOTHING. TL_RX_DATAPATH_CAP was requested, so a skip here would be a\
+ silently unconstrained lane. Refusing to continue. (Shipping build spells\
+ these gpiorx_<n>/link_data_pad_clk_reg[15:0], 16 per lane, 128 total.)"
+      }
+      if {[sizeof_collection $_dp_c] != 16} {
+          puts "**WARN: tidelink_constraints.sdc: lane $n capture flops bound\
+ [sizeof_collection $_dp_c], expected 16."
+      }
+      set_max_delay $D2D_RX_DP_MAX -ignore_clock_latency \
+          -from [get_ports "TL_RX\[$n\]"] -to $_dp_c
+      incr _dp_n
+  }
+  if {$_dp_n != 8} {
+      error "tidelink_constraints.sdc: applied $_dp_n/8 RX datapath caps."
+  }
+  puts "TIDELINK-SDC: RX per-lane pad->capture cap ACTIVE - $_dp_n lanes at\
+ $D2D_RX_DP_MAX ns (T_UI/5 = [expr {$D2D_T_UI / 5.0}] plus the\
+ $D2D_RX_IN_MAX ns input delay the tool charges at default globals)"
+  unset _dp_n _dp_c _c
+
+} else {
+  puts "TIDELINK-SDC: NOTE RX per-lane pad->capture cap NOT applied\
+ (TL_RX_DATAPATH_CAP unset). This is HOLE 4 and it is deliberately OPEN -\
+ see the block above for the hold interaction that has to be measured first."
+}
